==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gmh|de}}, from {{etyl|goh|de}} {{term|mahhōn|lang=goh}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*makōną}}; akin to Low German {{term|maken|lang=nds}}, Dutch {{term|maken|lang=nl}}, English {{term|make|lang=en}}, West Frisian {{term|meitsje|lang=fy}}. Ultimately from {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*mag-||to knead, mix, make}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈmaχn/|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-machen.ogg|audio (Germany)}}
* {{audio|De-at-machen.ogg|audio (Austria and Southern Germany)}}
* {{rhymes|aχn|lang=de}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-weak|macht|machte|gemacht}}

# {{context|transitive|lang=de}} to [[do]], to [[make]] {{qualifier|to perform an action}}
#: '''''Mach''' es!'' — '''Do''' it!
#: ''Er '''hat''' eine große [[Schweinerei]] '''gemacht'''.'' — He '''has made''' a big mess.
#: ''Das '''hat''' er ganz allein '''gemacht'''!'' — He '''has done''' that all by himself!
# {{context|transitive|lang=de}} to [[make]]; to [[produce]]
#: ''Ich '''hab''' dir einen Kuchen '''gemacht'''!'' — I '''have made''' you a pie!
# {{context|transitive|lang=de}} to [[take]] (a [[photo]])
# {{context|transitive|lang=de}} to [[prepare]] {{qualifier|e.g. food, drinks}}
#: '''''Machst''' du heute das Essen?'' — '''Will''' you '''prepare''' dinner, today?
#: ''sich eine Pizza '''machen''''' — to '''prepare''' a pizza by oneself
# {{context|transitive|vernacular|lang=de}} to [[matter]]
#: ''Das '''macht''' nichts!'' — That doesn't '''matter'''!
# {{context|transitive|informal|colloquial|lang=de}} to [[come to]]; to [[total]]; to [[cost]] {{qualifier|prizes}}
#: ''Wie viel '''macht''' das?'' — How much '''does''' that '''come to'''?
# {{context|transitive|informal|colloquial|lang=de}} to [[earn]]; to [[profit]] {{qualifier|wages}}
#: ''Der Herr Müller ist echt reich; der '''macht''' mehr als 5000 im Monat.'' — Mr Müller is quite rich; he '''earns''' more than 5000 bucks per month.

====Usage notes====
Unlike English, the verbs {{term|machen|lang=de}} and {{term|tun|lang=de}} (cognates of English {{term|make|lang=en}} and {{term|do|lang=en}}, respectively) can be used synonymously in certain cases.

: If the verb implies an action, both ''machen'' and ''tun'' may be used interchangeably. ''Tun'' often implies an action with more serious consequences than ''machen''.
:: ''Ich bin mir nicht sicher, ob ich das '''tun'''/'''machen''' kann.'' — I am not sure if I can '''do''' that.
:: ''Wer hat sie umgebracht? Wer hat es '''getan'''?'' — Who has put her to death? Who has '''done''' it?

: But if there is something to be produced or made, ''machen'' ( like its cognate ''to make'' in English ) is preferred, instead.
:: ''Hast du den Kuchen '''gemacht'''? Er ist sehr lecker!.'' — Did you '''make''' the cake? It's delicious!.

